From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "D.R.racer" <drracer@seznam.cz>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:58:43 +0200
Subject: soc: sunxi: mbus: restore the DMA offset when a driver
 binds again

The MBUS notifier gives the MBUS masters their DMA offset (bus address
= physical address - PHYS_OFFSET) once, when the platform device is
added. The driver core frees dev->dma_range_map in
device_unbind_cleanup(), which runs when a driver unbinds and when a
probe fails or is deferred, and nothing sets the offset again. From
then on the device is handed CPU physical addresses.

On boards with 1 GiB of DRAM this goes unnoticed: the DRAM controller
decodes 30 address bits and the PHYS_OFFSET bit wraps away. On 2 GiB
boards such as the Cubietruck (A20) every access lands 1 GiB away from
the buffer. Reloading the cedrus module and encoding one frame was
enough to corrupt user memory (processes segfaulting, sshd killed);
lima would be hit the same way after a rebind.

Also handle BUS_NOTIFY_BIND_DRIVER. It is sent from driver_sysfs_add()
after dma_configure() and before the driver's probe, so the offset is
back in place before the driver allocates its first buffer. Skip
devices that already have a range map.

Tested on a Cubietruck (A20, 2 GiB): after rmmod/modprobe of
sunxi-cedrus the VE gets bus addresses again, and a lima unbind/bind
(used to recover from a GPU hang) brings the desktop back. Runs on a
Rikomagic MK802 (A10, 1 GiB) in the same kernel series, where the bug
cannot show.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/soc/sunxi/sunxi_mbus.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sunxi_mbus.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sunxi_mbus.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sunxi_mbus.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sunxi_mbus.c
@@ -65,7 +65,13 @@ static int sunxi_mbus_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb,
 	struct device *dev = __dev;
 	int ret;
 
-	if (event != BUS_NOTIFY_ADD_DEVICE)
+	/*
+	 * The driver core frees dev->dma_range_map when a driver unbinds
+	 * and when a probe fails or is deferred, so the offset set when the
+	 * device was added does not survive a module reload or a deferred
+	 * probe. Set it again right before every bind.
+	 */
+	if (event != BUS_NOTIFY_ADD_DEVICE && event != BUS_NOTIFY_BIND_DRIVER)
 		return NOTIFY_DONE;
 
 	/*
@@ -87,6 +93,9 @@ static int sunxi_mbus_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb,
 	if (of_property_present(dev->of_node, "interconnects"))
 		return NOTIFY_DONE;
 
+	if (dev->dma_range_map)
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
 	ret = dma_direct_set_offset(dev, PHYS_OFFSET, 0, SZ_4G);
 	if (ret)
 		dev_err(dev, "Couldn't setup our DMA offset: %d\n", ret);
-- 
